Phase 3: award and the cost plan
Each firm column in the price table has an Award button. It opens a two-step confirmation, and nothing is written until the PM confirms.
Step 1: choose what goes into the cost plan. A table lists every item the firm priced: scheduled stages, selected service options, and any extras offered unasked. Each row shows the firm's amount and a Cost plan line column.
• By default each scheduled item and selected option gets its own line, and extras are left out.
• The PM can tick extras in, or select several rows and choose Combine to roll them into one line with a name they type, for example Stages 3 to 5 into "Design development and documentation".
• A running total shows what will be written, and code checks it equals the sum of the included items to the cent.
Step 2: confirm. The preview shows the final parent and child lines, then one transaction writes:
• Parent line: the package's cost plan line, for example "Town planning: Firm A". Its contract value becomes the sum of its children. Its budget stays exactly as set; if none was set it stays blank and no variance is shown.
• Child lines: one per line chosen in step 1. Each remembers which schedule items it combines, so an invoice for "Stage 4" still finds the combined line that contains Stage 4.
• Package: status Awarded, linked to the firm as its supplier, with the firm's hourly rates stored for variations later.
• Other submissions: marked Unsuccessful and kept unchanged.
Award can be undone until an invoice is allocated to one of its child lines; after that the button explains why it is locked.
What this changes in the invoice spec. An invoice whose ABN matches an awarded firm settles its package by rule. The activity line becomes a Jev choice among that package's child lines only, usually a handful of options, so it is fast and confident. An invoice saying "Stage 2" lines up with the line holding the Stage 2 item the firm priced.
